 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q, so a long sequential screening cannot flush sheets that comparison work is actively reusing. Every newly admitted sheet enters `A1in`, a FIFO queue. A sheet promotes to `Am`, a separate LRU-ordered queue, the moment it is requested a second time while still resident. When the cache is over budget, eviction draws first from the head of `A1in` (oldest first-use sheet) and only draws from the tail of `Am` once `A1in` is empty, so one-off screening sheets are always cheaper to evict than a reused sheet. `A1in` is capped at a configurable fraction of the 12 GiB budget (default 25%, i.e. 3 GiB); `Am` takes the remainder. The split is a tuning parameter, not fixed by this design — see Open Questions. This policy, its queues, and the promotion counter are private replacement-tracking state inside the preview worker; the render queue, the dedup map, and the `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract are unaffected.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (`A1in` FIFO + `Am` LRU, promote on second reference) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparison of a small working set. LRU's low bookkeeping cost is outweighed by its vulnerability to a long screening evicting the comparison set outright. Aged frequency counts protect popular clips but add counter maintenance and can keep a prior project's favorites resident after an editor switches projects. 2Q isolates one-off screening sheets in `A1in` so they are always evicted before a reused sheet in `Am`, directly protecting comparison work during big screenings, at the accepted cost of two queues and a size split to tune. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. `A1in`/`Am` size split: defaults to 25%/75% of the 12 GiB budget. No production workload data yet justifies a different split; revisit once real screening/comparison mixes are observed.
